I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the reusable analysis scripts first, then build a chemically balanced phase inventory before committing anything to refinement. I’ll keep candidate polymorphs as competitors and validate every retained CIF both before fitting and after the final replay.

The chemistry strongly suggests lead tungstates, but PbO–WO₃ has several charge-balanced products (PbWO₄, Pb₂WO₅, Pb₃WO₆) and unreacted oxides are plausible at 500 °C. I’m now extracting peak families and checking the local COD inventory rather than assuming the nominal 1:1 product.

The peak inventory shows two distinct families already: a scheelite-like PbWO₄ family (including the dominant ≈27.81°, 45.13°, and high-angle sequence) and the characteristic three-line monoclinic WO₃ cluster near 23.5–24.8°. I’m screening exact records and precursor oxides now; the offset and widths will be judged only after a common instrumental model is fitted.

After applying the common ≈+0.42° angular offset, a third family is clear: tetragonal Pb₃O₄ lines map at 26.81°, 31.22°, 32.55°, 50.38°, 52.52°, 58.54°, and 66.40°. PbO remains only a watch-list candidate because its strongest lines overlap and several expected secondary lines are weak or absent. I’ll now test the three-phase inventory and non‑1:1 lead tungstate analogues quantitatively.

The first full three-phase Rietveld baseline converged at Rwp ≈ 7.93% (GOF ≈ 3.19) with approximate crystalline fractions 61.0% PbWO₄, 25.3% WO₃, and 13.7% Pb₃O₄. That is chemically coherent, but not yet an acceptable stopping point: the common profile collapsed to a largely Lorentzian width and the counting-statistics GOF is still high. I’m checking phase-resolved peak widths and structured residuals before testing size/strain and the PbO watch list.

The width check confirms the main residual cause: the common model predicts the two isolated WO₃ peaks at ≈23.53° and 24.02° with FWHM ≈0.25°, while observed FWHM is ≈0.13°; PbWO₄ widths are already close. I’m replaying anchored models where sharp WO₃ defines the common instrument width and only the broader phases receive either crystallite-size or microstrain broadening.

The targeted result is decisive: phase-specific size broadening lowers Rwp from 7.92% to about 6.30%, whereas strain alone only reaches 7.77% and drives the PbWO₄ strain to its zero bound. The replay-stable interpretation is therefore sharp monoclinic WO₃ plus size-broadened PbWO₄/Pb₃O₄; unsupported strain will be pruned. Next I’m challenging that model with polymorph/source alternatives, PbO, and charge-balanced Pb₂WO₅/Pb₃WO₆ analogues.

A fourth phase is now supported: the simple ambient orthorhombic massicot PbO model gives a replay-stable ≈3.0 wt% fraction and lowers Rwp from 6.76% to 5.94%; tetragonal litharge is driven to zero. The long-period incommensurate PbO record also fits, but the simpler passing ambient record fits better, so I’m retaining the latter and re-challenging the non‑1:1 tungstates against this expanded inventory.

The massicot assignment is supported by a sequence of independent positive residuals (not just its strongest overlap), while the Pb₂WO₅/Pb₃WO₆ analogues predict several strong lines where the four-phase baseline is already negative. Their small fitted scales are therefore being treated as residual-fitting artifacts, not additional phases. Background, polarization, and surface-roughness replays are now being compared before any texture term is considered.

The angular-intensity mismatch is explained overwhelmingly by polarization: refining one physically plausible effective-polarization parameter converges reproducibly to ≈0.98 and drops Rwp to 3.61% (GOF ≈1.45). Roughness is not complementary—it pushes both roughness and polarization to bounds—so it will remain neutral. I’m now checking whether the remaining low-angle phase residuals come from zero/borrowed displacement parameters or genuine texture before finalizing.

The remaining texture tests converge essentially to March ratios of 0.994–1.004 and do not materially improve the fit, so texture will be pruned. Restrained phase-wide isotropic displacement parameters are replay-stable and reduce the valid, bound-pruned fit to Rwp ≈3.08% and GOF ≈1.24. I’m doing the required final watch-list challenge under this exact model, then I’ll freeze, reopen, replay, export, and cross-check every deliverable.

The four dominant phases are stable, and the remaining decision is a weak but statistically supported Pb₃WO₆-like phase. I’m refining that analogue as a fifth phase under the same restrained profile model, then I’ll replay the project, re-test the rejected watch-list phases, and freeze only the terms that survive.

The weak phase remains active through the first coupled replays; this refinement is slower because its Pnma analogue has many symmetry-expanded sites tied to one displacement parameter. I’m checking whether that parameter and the phase scale settle away from their bounds before accepting it.

The weak phase’s free displacement parameter ran to its upper bound and inflated the fraction, so I have pruned that unsupported term. With a fixed, conventional \(U_\mathrm{iso}=0.010\ \text{Å}^2\), the phase is 1.35 wt% and still gives a large replay-stable fit improvement; adding its own size term gives no improvement. I’ll retain it provisionally as “Pb₃WO₆-like (Pnma)” and quantify the assignment uncertainty in the report.

The retained model now reopens and replays cleanly over the full 9.90–100.09° range: \(R_\mathrm{wp}=2.9948\%\), GOF \(=1.2079\), with all five fractions stable and summing to one. The alternate PbO, Pb₂WO₅ and cubic Pb₃WO₆ models return zero scale; the reviewed Pb₇W₈O₂₈.₈ model gives only a marginal gain while its strongest line lies on a negative residual, so it is rejected.

Completed and validated the five-phase refinement:

- PbWO₄ scheelite: 54.32 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃: 23.29 wt%
- Tetragonal Pb₃O₄: 17.71 wt%
- Orthorhombic PbO massicot: 3.33 wt%
- Pb₃WO₆-like Pnma phase: 1.35 wt% — tentative and semiquantitative

Final fit: Rwp 2.9948%, Rp 2.2931%, GOF 1.2079. The reopened project converged, fractions sum to one, and all final CIFs passed the health check.

